Bugfix: Make `prune` reject plans computed against a changed repository

The prune command recorded the snapshots, index files and pack files it used
to decide which data is still needed only once, while the actual deletions
and repacking happened later. If another `backup` finished in between (for
example when locks were bypassed with `--no-lock` or a stale lock was
removed), prune could delete pack files that the new snapshot had just
started to reference, or fail with a missing pack error. A dry-run could
also report a different set of objects than the subsequent real run.

Restic now records the exact repository state a prune plan is based on and
re-checks it immediately before modifying anything. If snapshots, index
files or pack files were added in the meantime, prune aborts with a clear
error without deleting any data and can be rerun. Dry-run and a real run
now derive their decisions through the same code path on the same state.

https://github.com/restic/restic/issues/22041
